### 6. Tests related to owner creation & duplicate detection

**The target test — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/Cp35Tests.java`** (full, this is what your change must make pass):

```java
/** soft-match: a new owner that is not a hard duplicate but shares an existing owner's
 * lastName and postcode (different telephone) is still created, with 'possibleDuplicate' true and
 * 'possibleDuplicateOf' set to the matching owner id. */
@Tag("cp35")
class Cp35Tests extends AcceptanceBase {

    @Test
    void coreFlagsPossibleDuplicate() throws Exception {
        String lastName = uniqueLastName();
        ObjectNode a = withPostcode(ownerNode());
        a.put("lastName", lastName);
        int ida = createOwnerOk(a);
        ObjectNode b = withPostcode(ownerNode()); // same postcode + lastName, unique telephone/address
        b.put("lastName", lastName);
        int idb = createOwnerOk(b);
        getOwner(idb).andExpect(jsonPath("$.possibleDuplicate").value(true))
                .andExpect(jsonPath("$.possibleDuplicateOf").value(ida));
    }
}
```

Semantics from this test: owner B shares owner A's `lastName` + `postcode` (both "2000" via `withPostcode`) but has a unique telephone and address. B is still created (2xx), and on GET, `possibleDuplicate == true` and `possibleDuplicateOf == ida` (the id of the earlier matching owner). Note the flag/reference are read back via a **separate GET** request, which strongly implies the values must be **persisted** on the Owner (not just derived from live siblings), and `possibleDuplicateOf` points to the *first/earliest* matching existing owner.

